Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "provisional-government-free-india":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Provisional Government of Free India",infoboxTitle:"Provisional Government of Free India",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind is an established eastern Indian successor power with extensive Japanese support, Calcutta as its primary administrative capital, and a claim to Delhi as its proper capital.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its continuity from the historical Provisional Government of Free India, retention of that formal title, inherited institutional apparatus, and permanent provisional constitution are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind's existence, strong Japanese material support, administrative capital at Calcutta, claim to Delhi as its proper capital, and resulting national grievance are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1943 institutional lineage, preterritorial apparatus, entry into India as an existing government, permanent formal provisional status, strong executive, secular national law, cultural federalism, planning system, constitutional deferral, chambers, succession, and suffrage are incorporated extrapolation or remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"proclamation",title:"Working preterritorial lineage",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>Continuity from the historical exile government through the <a href='indian-national-army-azad-hind.html'>Indian National Army</a>, Indian Independence League, Azad Hind Dal, <a href='founding-institutions-azad-hind.html'>Azad Hind Bank, and Rani of Jhansi organization</a> is incorporated extrapolation based on historical institutions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author does not directly establish their postwar legal continuity.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"entry",title:"Working entry into eastern India",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind becomes an eastern successor power and receives substantial Japanese support.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Entry as an already existing sovereign claimant, bargaining with or defeating local authorities, and rejection of a Japanese-created client cabinet are compatible state-formation detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"executive",title:"Working strong executive",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>A system combining Bose's inheritance, national planning, army centrality, mass mobilization, and a powerful executive, with later consultative institutions subordinate to liberation, is incorporated political extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its constitutional form is not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"nation",title:"Working political nation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>A secular pan-Indian national law rejecting exclusive Hindu, Muslim, Bengali, communist, and princely sovereignties while granting cultural and administrative autonomy is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The established Delhi claim supports an all-Indian ambition but does not settle this constitutional program.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"planning",title:"Working planning and mobilization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>Central direction of transport, currency, military command, industrial priorities, and development as material independence is compatible with Azad Hind's military position.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes Japanese matériel dependence and later domestic munitions development, not this complete planning system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"provisional",title:"Working permanent provisional status",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>Retaining the provisional title and deferring a final constitution until Delhi is recovered is incorporated extrapolation from the established capital claim.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes Delhi as the proper capital and a national grievance but does not specify this legal mechanism.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"calcutta",title:"Government in Calcutta",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p><a href='calcutta-delhi-dual-capital.html'>Calcutta</a> is Azad Hind's primary administrative capital, while Delhi is claimed as the proper capital.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Ministries, barracks, embassies, planning offices, central finance, monumental works, and official temporary language are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Working emergency order",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p5l6j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:56:00-06:00 --><p>Using the reversals of 1956 and 1965 to justify continuity, expanded councils, delayed demobilization, and constitutional deferral is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The wars and unresolved Delhi claim are established, but this institutional response is not.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"calcutta-delhi-dual-capital.html",kicker:"Constitutional geography",label:"Calcutta–Delhi Dual Capital"},{href:"founding-institutions-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Institutional inheritance",label:"Founding Institutions of Azad Hind"}],
    facts:[["Established state","Azad Hind"],["Established administrative capital","Calcutta"],["Established claimed proper capital","Delhi"],["Established external support","Japan"],["Provisional-government lineage and 1943 origin","Working reconstruction"],["Political and constitutional order","Working reconstruction"],["Final constitution and leadership succession","Open"]]
  }),

  "indian-national-army-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Indian National Army in Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Azad Hind National Army",infoboxKicker:"Founding military estate",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind fields one of the strongest early Indian successor-state forces because Japan supplies matériel, including a limited armored capability unavailable to many neighbors.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Continuity from the wartime Indian National Army, its status as a founding military estate, and veteran influence over the postwar army and state are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind's substantial Japanese military support, armored advantage, early internal pacification, campaigns against Congress India, 1956 supply-driven reversal, later domestic munitions effort, and participation in the northern long war are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The wartime INA's legal continuity, founding-estate role, recruitment streams, veteran privileges, command dominance, regional composition, women's service tradition, final title, units, strength, ranks, commanders, and civil–military law are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Working revolutionary inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>The wartime INA entering India as the armed force of the <a href='provisional-government-free-india.html'>Provisional Government</a>, with its campaigns, prisoners, sacrifices, and oath forming the state's principal founding memory, is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Postwar institutional continuity is not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"expansion",title:"Working expansion into a national army",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>Recruitment from local volunteers, former British Indian personnel, Bengali and Assamese formations, regional militias, Japanese-trained officers, and overseas Indian communities is compatible army-building detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author does not specify these streams.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"veterans",title:"Working veteran estate",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>A mid-1950s army numerically dominated by postwar entrants but led by original INA veterans who receive public-employment and ceremonial preference is incorporated institutional extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No strength or privilege system is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"regions",title:"Working pan-Indian force",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>A pan-Indian claim combined with eastern replacement manpower, regional formations, territorial forces, and a central officer corps suspicious of provincial armies is compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The established army's territorial and regional composition remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese support and limits",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>Japan supplies Azad Hind with matériel, including Chi-Nu, Chi-To, and limited Chi-Ri tanks, giving it an important early capability but not invulnerability; dependence on the Burma supply route causes the 1956 ammunition crisis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Staff training, communications practice, aviation integration, and institutional limits are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"campaigns",title:"Established campaigns",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind first pacifies internal territory, including the Assam corridor and Bangladeshi population, advances against Congress India from 1952 through 1955, retreats after its ammunition crisis in 1956, and later fights in the northern long war.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact units and campaign organization remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"society",title:"Working army and society",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>Permanent mobilization, conscription, armed civilians, and guerrilla-defense preparation are established across the northern states.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Veterans' halls, military schools, public works, and a Rani of Jhansi lineage in medicine, communications, logistics, civil defense, and officer formation are incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"institution",title:"Working national doctrine",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-i6m7j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:57:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind develops domestic munitions after the 1956 supply crisis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> An <a href='azad-hind-armoured-school.html'>Azad Hind Armoured School</a> converting the losses of 1956 and 1963–65 into doctrine for logistics, recovery, engineers, infantry cooperation, and production is compatible institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"azad-hind-armored-forces.html",kicker:"Combat arm",label:"Armored Forces of Azad Hind"},{href:"azad-hind-armoured-school.html",kicker:"Postwar doctrine",label:"Azad Hind Armoured School"}],
    facts:[["Established state force","Azad Hind army"],["Established external supplier","Japan"],["Established early advantage","Limited armor and coherent matériel supply"],["Established limit","1956 ammunition crisis and reversal"],["Wartime INA continuity and founding status","Working reconstruction"],["Recruitment, command, veteran, and women's institutions","Working reconstruction"],["Exact title, units, ranks, commanders, and strength","Open"]]
  }),

  "founding-institutions-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Founding Institutions of Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Founding institutions of Azad Hind",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 -->The Indian Independence League, Azad Hind Dal, Azad Hind Bank, and Rani of Jhansi organization are historical starting points for a possible political, financial, diasporic, and national-service apparatus in territorial Azad Hind.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their survival and institutional roles after the Breaking of the Raj are incorporated reconstruction, not direct authorial establishment.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 -->The named organizations, their preterritorial historical existence, survival into the territorial state, postwar political and financial functions, diaspora network, national-service role, mergers, charters, leadership, finances, membership, and ministry relationships are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes Azad Hind as an Indian successor power but does not directly define this institutional inheritance.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"system",title:"Working preterritorial apparatus",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>Political leagues collecting support, a bank handling funds, cadres organizing administration, and a women's regiment supplying a national-service tradition before territorial rule form a compatible institutional bundle.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its transfer into post-Raj government is not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"league",title:"Working Indian Independence League",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>The League's evolution into a state network for fundraising, commerce, propaganda, overseas Indian communities, and liaison throughout Japanese-led Asia is incorporated extrapolation from its historical wartime role.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"dal",title:"Working Azad Hind Dal",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>The Dal supplying disciplined cadres for local organization, campaigning, recruitment, ceremony, and provincial administration is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its continuation and relationship to elections or parties remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bank",title:"Working Azad Hind Bank",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>The bank becoming the central bank or its institutional ancestor, linking currency, veteran funds, reconstruction credit, subscriptions, and economic planning, is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its territorial legal form remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"rani",title:"Working Rani of Jhansi organization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>Survival as a women's military and national-service institution in medicine, communications, logistics, civil defense, training, and ceremony is incorporated extrapolation from the wartime regiment.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Neither continuation nor service scope is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"diaspora",title:"Working overseas network",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>League and bank channels moving money, technicians, volunteers, commercial intelligence, and family connections between Azad Hind and overseas Indian communities are compatible extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The postwar network is not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"territory",title:"Working transformation under territorial rule",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>Revolutionary bodies coexisting with ministries, police, courts, and local councils, while founding prestige prevents easy abolition or absorption, is compatible institutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No merger or survival pattern is fixed.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Working institutional memory",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f7n8j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:58:30-06:00 --><p>National ritual using these bodies to claim indigenous revolutionary continuity rather than post-conquest manufacture is compatible political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes Japanese support but does not directly establish this legitimacy argument.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"provisional-government-free-india.html",kicker:"State authority",label:"Provisional Government of Free India"},{href:"indian-national-army-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Military institution",label:"Indian National Army in Azad Hind"}],
    facts:[["Working political network","Indian Independence League"],["Working cadre organization","Azad Hind Dal"],["Working financial institution","Azad Hind Bank"],["Working women's institution","Rani of Jhansi organization"],["Historical origin and territorial continuity","Incorporated reconstruction"],["Postwar functions and legal forms","Open"]]
  }),

  "calcutta-delhi-dual-capital":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Calcutta–Delhi Constitutional Duality",infoboxTitle:"Calcutta and Delhi",infoboxKicker:"Administrative and promised capitals",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 -->Calcutta is Azad Hind's primary administrative capital, but the state claims Delhi as its proper capital; the mismatch is an established national grievance, explicitly compared by the author to West Germany's capital claim.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A formal constitutional duality expressed through law, ritual, architecture, and military culture is incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 -->Calcutta's administrative role, Delhi's claimed proper-capital status, and the resulting grievance are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Permanent administrative language, a lawful or promised constitutional formula, survival and uses of <em>Chalo Delhi</em>, deliberate constitutional incompleteness, temporary rhetoric around Calcutta's buildings, a future Delhi parliament, exact clauses, plans, and designs are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"formula",title:"Administrative and claimed capitals",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Calcutta is the primary administrative capital, while Delhi is claimed as Azad Hind's proper capital.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Ministries, missions, army headquarters, finance, and planning in Calcutta, and a lawful promised-capital formula involving rival <a href='hindustan.html'>Hindustan</a>, are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"chalo",title:"Working Chalo Delhi tradition",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Survival of <em>Chalo Delhi</em> on monuments, currency, schools, stations, veterans' halls, ceremonies, and seals as a constitutional claim is incorporated extrapolation from the established grievance.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its postwar official use is not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"architecture",title:"Working provisional architecture",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Calcutta receiving parade avenues and monumental ministries while officials avoid treating them as the final national capitol is compatible architectural extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No building program or official vocabulary is specified.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"parliament",title:"Working parliament promised to Delhi",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>A permanent parliamentary complex reserved for Delhi after liberation, allowing the executive to frame constitutional incompleteness as fidelity, is incorporated political extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Neither parliament nor design is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"war",title:"Delhi as strategic objective",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind's Delhi claim and westward 1952–56 advance against Congress India are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Calling the campaign the <a href='delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html'>Delhi Campaign</a> and requiring later limited offensives to enact the claim are compatible interpretations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"rivals",title:"Working rival claims",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Congress India and Hindustan are established as separate rival Indian projects, and Azad Hind claims Delhi.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Assigning Congress a secular-continuity doctrine, Hindustan a Hindu-majoritarian possession claim, and Azad Hind a synthesis of geography and legality is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"daily",title:"Working everyday duality",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Generations living in an officially interim capital, with the tension appearing in addresses, stationery, commemorations, and disputes over improving Calcutta, is compatible social extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No such custom or debate is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"Working constitutional mobilization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c8p9j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:00:00-06:00 --><p>Using the capital mismatch to sustain political cohesion, constitutional incompleteness, and mobilization after defeat is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly establishes the grievance but not its legal consequences.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"provisional-government-free-india.html",kicker:"Constitutional order",label:"Provisional Government of Free India"},{href:"delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html",kicker:"Military claim",label:"Delhi Campaign"}],
    facts:[["Established administrative capital","Calcutta"],["Established claimed proper capital","Delhi"],["Established political effect","National grievance"],["Formal lawful or promised status","Working reconstruction"],["Chalo Delhi, provisional rhetoric, and future parliament","Working reconstruction"],["Exact constitutional settlement and designs","Open"]]
  }),

  "eastern-consolidation-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Eastern Consolidation of Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Eastern consolidation",infoboxKicker:"Territorial formation · 1948–1952",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 -->Before about 1952, Azad Hind is principally occupied with pacifying its own territory. Its armored advantage helps subdue the Assam corridor and secure the Bangladeshi population, while Calcutta serves as its administrative capital and Japanese matériel sustains its forces.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A named 1948–52 eastern consolidation across Bengal, Orissa, and Assam is incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 -->Azad Hind's pre-1952 internal pacification, Calcutta base, use of armor to subdue the Assam corridor and secure the Bangladeshi population, Japanese support, and later contact with communist forces around the Orissa–Sambalpur area are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1948–52 periodization and event title, an autonomous East Bengal settlement, corridor-centered administration, complete Orissa integration, bargaining methods, four-part formula, campaigns, borders, coalitions, forces, casualties, and completion date are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"problem",title:"Pacification before westward war",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Before about 1952, Azad Hind concentrates on pacifying its internal components rather than fighting heavily with its principal external rivals.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Securing food, taxation, transport, ports, police, and recruitment while bargaining with local authorities and armed groups is compatible state-building detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"calcutta",title:"Calcutta as organizing center",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Calcutta is Azad Hind's primary administrative capital.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Cadres, port facilities, finance, symbolism, communications, and authority extending through rail and river nodes are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bengal",title:"Working East Bengal bargain",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind secures the Bangladeshi population during its internal pacification.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A Dacca-centered <a href='east-bengal-administration.html'>autonomous administration</a> with language protection, police, territorial forces, and land and education powers is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"assam",title:"Control of the Assam corridor",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind uses its armored capability to subdue the Assam corridor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Rule following railways, rivers, tea, oil, towns, roads, and airfields while hill chiefs, councils, administrations, and insurgents retain local substance is compatible territorial reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"orissa",title:"Working Orissa depth",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind and communist forces meet around Sambalpur and the Mahanadi in Orissa.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Easier political integration, poverty, uneven administration, ports, mines, workshops, roads, and strategic depth are incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"methods",title:"Working methods of consolidation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Political guarantees, incorporation of former British Indian personnel, territorial forces, tax agreements, police-building, and selective campaigning are compatible methods.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes pacification and corridor control but not these administrative mechanisms.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese material support",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>Japanese matériel, especially scarce armored vehicles, gives Azad Hind an early advantage while the Burma supply route creates structural ammunition dependence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Specific training, advisory, aviation, and communications roles are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"result",title:"Working territorial formula",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e9q0j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:01:15-06:00 --><p>A four-part formula in which Bengal supplies population and administration, Orissa provides depth and resources, Assam carries the eastern corridor, and Japan provides modern matériel is incorporated synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The shift toward westward war around 1952 is established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"east-bengal-administration.html",kicker:"Constituent settlement",label:"East Bengal Autonomous Administration"},{href:"assam-corridor-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Strategic spine",label:"Assam Corridor"},{href:"orissa-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Southern depth",label:"Orissa in Azad Hind"}],
    facts:[["Established phase","Internal pacification before about 1952"],["Established political center","Calcutta"],["Established Assam result","Corridor subdued with armored advantage"],["Established Bengal result","Bangladeshi population secured"],["Established Orissa contact","Communist frontier near Sambalpur and the Mahanadi"],["Established foreign matériel","Japan"],["Periodization, institutions, borders, and methods","Working reconstruction or open"]]
  }),

  "east-bengal-administration":azadHindArticle({
    title:"East Bengal Autonomous Administration",infoboxTitle:"East Bengal Autonomous Administration",infoboxKicker:"Azad Hind constituent territory",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 -->Azad Hind secures the Bangladeshi population during its early internal pacification, and the 1991 Bangladesh cyclone strikes territory it governs.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A Dacca-centered autonomous East Bengal administration balancing local cultural powers against Calcutta's strategic authority is incorporated political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 -->The Bangladeshi population's inclusion in Azad Hind and the use of Bangladesh as a geographic descriptor are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Dacca's autonomous administration, Bengali public language, Muslim religious and family-law protections, local police and territorial formations, land and education powers, Calcutta's retained strategic powers, Bangladesh as a cultural but non-sovereign constitutional name, formal title, boundaries, chamber, elections, officeholders, and later crises are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"necessity",title:"Working rationale for autonomy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind's need to secure a Bangladeshi population is established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A Dacca-centered Bengali Muslim majority receiving autonomy to avoid conquest or separation within a noncommunal Bose state is incorporated political extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"dacca",title:"Working government in Dacca",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>A locally elected or corporative Dacca government with substantial provincial authority is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its existence, chamber, franchise, and powers are not directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"language",title:"Working Bengali public life",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Bengali as the principal local language of administration, education, and politics within multilingual national institutions is compatible extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No language law or sovereignty formula is fixed.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"religion",title:"Working religious-law guarantees",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Protection of Muslim religious institutions and personal or family law within a secular national system is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Neither the national legal order nor these guarantees are directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"local",title:"Working local powers",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Local control of ordinary land and education policy, police, and territorial formations is incorporated constitutional extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes inclusion of the population but not the division of powers.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"calcutta",title:"Working central powers",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Calcutta is Azad Hind's established administrative capital.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Reserving foreign affairs, heavy command, interstate transport, currency, planning, and final constitutional authority to the center is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bangladesh",title:"Working Bangladesh identity",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>The author uses Bangladeshi and Bangladesh for the population and cyclone zone within Azad Hind.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A common cultural name deliberately denied sovereign-republic status is incorporated constitutional interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"tension",title:"Working autonomy under mobilization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b0r1j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:02:45-06:00 --><p>Separatism, communal fear, recruitment and resource disputes, and a bargain dependent on Calcutta respecting local authority are compatible political consequences.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No autonomy crisis or settlement durability is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"eastern-consolidation-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Founding campaign",label:"Eastern Consolidation"},{href:"disaster-relief-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Later civil system",label:"Disaster Relief in Azad Hind"}],
    facts:[["Established state","Azad Hind"],["Established population and geographic descriptor","Bangladeshi; Bangladesh"],["Dacca administrative center and autonomy","Working reconstruction"],["Language, religious law, local and central powers","Working reconstruction"],["Formal title, borders, institutions, and later crises","Open"]]
  }),

  "assam-corridor-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Assam Corridor under Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Assam corridor",infoboxKicker:"Eastern strategic territory",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 -->Azad Hind uses its scarce Japanese-supplied armored capability to subdue the Assam corridor, and its ammunition supply reaches the state overland through Burma until the Burmese Federal Crisis disrupts the route.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> An integrated network of railways, rivers, tea, oil, towns, roads, and airfields controlled more fully than the surrounding hill country is incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 -->Azad Hind's subjugation of the Assam corridor using its armored advantage, Japanese supply through Burma, interruption during the 1955 Burmese Federal Crisis, and resulting 1956 ammunition shortage are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Corridor-centered civil rule, listed economic assets, hill chiefs and councils, insurgency, valley doctrine, detailed route chain, military administration, exact borders, local governments, forces, and legal settlement are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"network",title:"Working strategic network",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>The Assam corridor is an established object of Azad Hind's pacification.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Authority following railways, river landings, tea, oil, towns, roads, airfields, trains, and depots more strongly than ordinary rural administration is compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"valleys",title:"Working valley operations",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind's armored capability helps subdue the corridor.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Infantry, armor, police, and engineers securing valley junctions, bridges, depots, and extraction districts is compatible operational elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"hills",title:"Working hill autonomy and insurgency",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Hill peoples retaining chiefs, councils, or emergency administrations, with cooperation and insurgency beyond the corridor and armor unable to replace political bargains, is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No hill settlement or insurgent movement is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"resources",title:"Working tea, oil, and transport system",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Tea exports, oil for mechanization, river transport, airfields, and stronger protection for strategic assets than surrounding administration are compatible geographic and economic detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> These assets are not enumerated by the author.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"burma",title:"The Burma supply route",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Japanese matériel and especially ammunition reach Azad Hind through an overland Burma route selected partly to avoid maritime interdiction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A precise chain through Burmese ports, depots, crossings, Assam, Bengal railways, and front-line nodes is incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"1956",title:"The route breaks",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>The 1955 Burmese Federal Crisis interrupts supply; residual matériel and ammunition sustain much of that year, but by 1956 shortages contribute to failed Azad Hind attacks and a Congress counteroffensive.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Particular detours, losses to local forces, and route timings remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"security",title:"Working military administration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Railway guards, road engineers, depot commands, territorial formations, and airfield security exercising unusual authority to preserve throughput are compatible institutional extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No corridor command or administrative regime is specified.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"Working corridor-state interpretation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a1s2j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:04:10-06:00 --><p>Interpreting the Assam settlement as control of movement rather than uniform country, within a culturally federal but strategically centralized state, is incorporated synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Only subjugation of the corridor and its supply importance are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"eastern-consolidation-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Territorial origin",label:"Eastern Consolidation"},{href:"burma-assam-ammunition-corridor.html",kicker:"Wartime logistics",label:"Burma–Assam Ammunition Corridor"}],
    facts:[["Established state","Azad Hind"],["Established result","Assam corridor subdued with armored advantage"],["Established supply connection","Japanese matériel through Burma"],["Established decisive crisis","1955 disruption and 1956 ammunition shortage"],["Assets, civil rule, hill institutions, and military administration","Working reconstruction"],["Borders, governments, forces, and legal settlement","Open"]]
  }),

  "orissa-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Orissa in Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Orissa",infoboxKicker:"Southern depth and resource province",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 -->Azad Hind and communist forces meet in Orissa, especially around Sambalpur and the Mahanadi, marking a southern edge of Azad Hind's expansion.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Orissa as an integrated province supplying ports, mines, workshops, roads, and strategic depth to Calcutta is incorporated territorial and economic reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 -->Azad Hind's presence in Orissa and its recurring contact with communist forces around Sambalpur and the Mahanadi are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Inclusion in a 1948–52 eastern consolidation, complete provincial integration, comparative political ease, poverty, uneven administration, ports, minerals, workshops, roads, depth, planning, borders, constitution, capital, parties, production, and princely relationships are incorporated extrapolation or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"integration",title:"Working political integration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind occupies territory in Orissa and meets communist forces there.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Easier integration than East Bengal or the Assamese hills through bargains with officials, elites, former colonial personnel, and princely systems is incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"poverty",title:"Working administrative condition",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Poverty, uneven district administration, weak roads, health, land records, and police, and military or development agencies filling ministerial roles are compatible provincial detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author does not establish these conditions.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"ports",title:"Working Bay of Bengal ports",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Coastal facilities supplementing Calcutta, moving bulk goods and relief traffic, and connecting the economy to maritime trade while remaining less secure than the Burma route are compatible strategic geography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No specific port role is established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"minerals",title:"Working mines and materials",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Mineral districts supplying rail repair, construction, workshops, and later munitions production, with mine-to-plant transport treated as national security, are incorporated economic extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"workshops",title:"Working workshops and repair",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind develops domestic munitions after the 1956 foreign-ammunition crisis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Locating rail, motor, artillery, and industrial workshops in Orissa behind the Bengal front is compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"roads",title:"Working roads and western approaches",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Orissan roads providing depth south of the Ganges axis and linking Calcutta to Chota Nagpur and central India, with fortifications extending beyond Patna, are compatible geographic extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact routes and defenses remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"planning",title:"Working planned war economy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Central prioritization of power, extraction, workshops, depots, and transport, with local development judged by defense value, is incorporated political-economic extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No Orissan planning system is directly established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"role",title:"Working strategic-depth interpretation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-o2t3j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:05:40-06:00 --><p>Interpreting Orissa as the depth and resource base preventing Azad Hind from becoming a Bengal coastal enclave is incorporated synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes territorial contact with communist forces around Sambalpur and the Mahanadi, not this complete role.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"eastern-consolidation-azad-hind.html",kicker:"State formation",label:"Eastern Consolidation"},{href:"azad-hind-munitions-independence-program.html",kicker:"Industrial consequence",label:"Munitions Independence Program"}],
    facts:[["Established state presence","Azad Hind"],["Established opposing force","Communist forces"],["Established contact zone","Sambalpur and the Mahanadi"],["Integration chronology and provincial order","Working reconstruction"],["Ports, mines, workshops, roads, and strategic role","Working reconstruction"],["Borders, constitution, capital, parties, and production","Open"]]
  }),

  "delhi-campaign-1952-1956":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Delhi Campaign",eyebrow:"Indian Wars · Azad Hind offensive · 1952–1956",infoboxTitle:"Delhi Campaign",infoboxKicker:"Working name for Azad Hind's high-water campaign",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-d1q2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:04:12-06:00 -->From 1952 through 1956, Azad Hind pushes Congress India westward through the Ganges system with a Japanese matériel advantage. The Burmese Federal Crisis breaks the overland ammunition supply chain, under-supplied final attacks fail, and Congress drives Azad Hind back toward earlier depots near Bengal and Calcutta.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> <strong>Delhi Campaign</strong> is a compatible descriptive title rather than an author-selected contemporary name.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-d1q2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:04:12-06:00 -->The 1952–56 sequence, principal Azad Hind–Congress India conflict, westward advance through the Ganges plain, Japanese matériel and advisory advantage, 1955 Burmese route disruption, delayed 1956 ammunition crisis, ill-advised final attacks, Congress counteroffensive, and retreat toward earlier eastern positions and Calcutta are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Delhi Campaign as a formal name, precise Bihar route, foreign-observer expectations, exact high-water point, Congress wartime capital, “road to Delhi” slogan, “Delhi was lost at Rangoon” memory phrase, battles, commanders, casualties, and armistice date remain working or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"meeting",title:"1952: organized states meet",html:"<p>Azad Hind and Congress India spend the first post-Raj years consolidating their own rear areas. By 1952 their forces encounter one another as organized governments rather than loose coalitions of militias and inherited units.</p>"},
      {id:"tempo",title:"1953–1954: Japanese matériel sets the tempo",html:"<p>Tanks, artillery ammunition, radios, trucks, engineers, aircraft parts, medicine, advisers, and replacement weapons give Azad Hind the better integrated army. Congress has greater local manpower but a mixed material system.</p>"},
      {id:"advance",title:"The westward advance",html:"<p>Azad Hind pushes through Bihar and along Ganges transport routes, forcing Congress to abandon positions threatened by armored penetration or encirclement. By 1954 foreign observers increasingly consider Calcutta the likely northern victor.</p>"},
      {id:"highwater",title:"1955: the high-water mark",html:"<p>The advance approaches the central Ganges position and threatens Congress India's wartime center, plausibly around Allahabad or Lucknow. Propaganda declares that another campaign will open the road to Delhi; the exact Congress capital remains open.</p>"},
      {id:"corridor",title:"The Burma–Assam lifeline fails",html:"<p>The Burmese Federal Crisis disrupts road, rail, depot, and convoy systems. Supplies continue unevenly for months, allowing the army to advance while its replacement stream is already failing.</p>"},
      {id:"ammunition",title:"The ammunition crisis",html:"<p>By early 1956 artillery fire is rationed, tank high-explosive allocations fall, infantry machine-gun and mortar issues shrink, and forward formations consume defensive caches never intended to sustain a distant offensive.</p>"},
      {id:"final",title:"The final offensive and counterattack",html:"<p>Political pressure compels one last attack. Short artillery preparation, ammunition conservation, weak suppressive fire, fuel shortages, and failed recovery prevent exploitation. Congress counterattacks on shorter lines with locally available ammunition and increasing Russian-derived equipment.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Retreat and the lost-victory interpretation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-d1q2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:04:12-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind retreats toward earlier positions and ammunition caches nearer Bengal and Calcutta, and the loss becomes a major grievance against Burma.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Eastern Bihar and Chota Nagpur as the exact fallback line, a settled “lost victory” memory, and the phrases “Burma emptied its guns” and “Delhi was lost at Rangoon” are compatible narrative synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"proxy-high-tide.html",kicker:"Wider conflict period",label:"Proxy High Tide"},{href:"indian-arms-market.html",kicker:"Material setting",label:"Indian Arms Market"},{href:"azad-hind-lost-victory.html",kicker:"Political memory",label:"Azad Hind's Lost Victory"},{href:"burma-assam-ammunition-corridor.html",kicker:"Broken lifeline",label:"Burma–Assam Ammunition Corridor"},{href:"eastern-fortified-belt.html",kicker:"Postwar frontier",label:"Eastern Fortified Belt"}],
    facts:[["Period","1952–1956"],["Belligerents","Azad Hind and Congress India"],["Established axis","Westward through the Ganges plain"],["Established disruption","Burmese Federal Crisis begins breaking the supply route in 1955"],["Established 1956 crisis","Ammunition exhaustion and failed final attacks"],["Result","Congress counterattack; Azad Hind retreats toward eastern depots"],["Working article title","Delhi Campaign"],["Battles, high-water line, commanders, and losses","Open"]]
  }),

  "azad-hind-munitions-independence-program":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Azad Hind Munitions Independence Program",infoboxTitle:"Munitions Independence Program",infoboxKicker:"Working post-1956 industrial strategy",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z1r2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->Azad Hind's 1956 defeat demonstrates that its Japanese-supported army depends critically on ammunition moving through Burma. Supplies later resume, while Indian states gradually develop more of their own weapons and munitions capacity.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> An <strong>Azad Hind Munitions Independence Program</strong> prioritizing propellant, primers, barrels, tracks, and engine parts over a complete modern tank is compatible industrial reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z1r2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->The 1956 ammunition-dependency crisis, later resumption of supplies, and growth of domestic Indian arms and munitions capacity are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A discrete Azad Hind program, its immediate post-1956 origin, formal supply-independence objective, listed ammunition and component priorities, Calcutta–Orissa industrial network, Japanese assistance, retained technology ladder, and outcome are assistant synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Program existence, title, agencies, priorities, budgets, sites, output, licensing, foreign role, and dates remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"lesson",title:"The lesson of 1956",html:"<p>Azad Hind concludes that armor did not fail; foreign ammunition dependency failed. Replacement vehicles without shells, fuel, recovery parts, and artillery support could not restore the offensive once Burma became unreliable.</p>"},
      {id:"smallarms",title:"Cartridges and infantry supply",html:"<p>Rifle and machine-gun cartridges, mortar bombs, primers, propellant, and casings receive first priority because infantry and territorial forces consume them across every front and internal-security district.</p>"},
      {id:"artillery",title:"Artillery and tank ammunition",html:"<p>Domestic shell filling, case manufacture, propellant, and caliber standardization aim to prevent a repeat of the rationing sequence that destroyed the final Delhi offensive.</p>"},
      {id:"parts",title:"Barrels, tracks, and engine parts",html:"<p>The program expands repair and component capacity for spare barrels, tracks, running gear, and selected engine parts. Keeping an existing tank operational is treated as a more urgent form of sovereignty than unveiling a wholly national design.</p>"},
      {id:"plants",title:"An eastern industrial network",html:"<p>Calcutta's administration, Orissan minerals and workshops, Bengal railways, and military depots form the likely production system. Exact plant sites and ministry boundaries remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese assistance after humiliation",html:"<p>Japanese advisers who built the army share the humiliation of watching supply failure defeat it. Tokyo tolerates and supports parts of the program because a reliable associated arsenal reduces the future burden on Japan.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"The retained technology ladder",html:"<p>Japanese firms continue to control newer vehicles, advanced optics, engines, and designs. Azad Hind becomes less dependent in consumption and maintenance without becoming equal to Japan in complete systems.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Industrial sovereignty in limited form",html:"<p>The program allows the state to support older Japanese-derived fleets, sustain fortifications, and enter later wars with deeper domestic stocks. Its governing principle is endurance: no single foreign corridor should again silence the whole army.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html",kicker:"Originating defeat",label:"Delhi Campaign"},{href:"azad-hind-armored-forces.html",kicker:"Principal user",label:"Armored Forces of Azad Hind"},{href:"orissa-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Industrial depth",label:"Orissa in Azad Hind"}],
    facts:[["Established lesson","Foreign ammunition dependency breaks the 1956 offensive"],["Established later trend","Supplies resume and domestic Indian arms capacity grows"],["Working program","Azad Hind Munitions Independence Program"],["Working priorities","Ammunition, propellant, primers, barrels, tracks, and engine parts"],["Working foreign partner","Japan"],["Program, agencies, sites, outputs, and dates","Open"]]
  }),

  "azad-hind-armored-forces":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Armored Forces of Azad Hind",infoboxTitle:"Armored forces of Azad Hind",infoboxKicker:"Japanese-derived armored arm",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z2s3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->Japanese-supplied Chi-Nu and Chi-To tanks, with smaller numbers of Chi-Ri vehicles including Eastern Siberian veterans, give Azad Hind an armored capability many neighbors initially lack.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The force's exact organization, 120–180 high-water estimate, “Siberians” nickname, limited Type 16 Shu-Ha modernization, and detailed combined-arms system are compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z2s3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->Azad Hind use of Chi-Nu, Chi-To, and small numbers of Chi-Ri tanks; transfer of some vehicles with Eastern Siberian service markings; an early capability advantage; decisive Japanese ammunition dependency; later receipt and heavy loss of advanced postwar Japanese tanks; and the resulting decision to invest in a tank school are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact vehicle roles, the “Siberians” nickname and honor system, 120–180 fleet estimate, Type 16 Shu-Ha identification, transfer schedule, organization, doctrine, unit names, and inventories are working synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"fleet",title:"The early Japanese fleet",html:"<p>Type 3 Chi-Nu tanks serve in support and second-line roles, Type 4 Chi-To vehicles form the principal modern medium fleet, and limited Type 5 Chi-Ri tanks support command and breakthrough work.</p>"},
      {id:"siberians",title:"Eastern Siberian veterans",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z2s3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 --><p>Some transferred vehicles served in the Japanese conquest of Eastern Siberia and reach Azad Hind with markings from that service.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Incomplete repainting, preserved plaques or badges, battle-honor practice, and the <strong>Siberians</strong> nickname were supplied by the assistant.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"scale",title:"A concentrated force",html:"<p>A working high-water estimate places roughly 120–180 serviceable tanks in the main army by 1954–55, enough for small armored groups, independent battalions, and training pools. The figure remains working rather than fixed canon.</p>"},
      {id:"system",title:"A system rather than a collection",html:"<p>Azad Hind can concentrate functioning tanks with fuel, ammunition, radios, recovery, trained crews, and supporting infantry. Rivals may own comparable paper inventories but cannot necessarily assemble them into one operational force.</p>"},
      {id:"uses",title:"Assam and the Ganges plain",html:"<p>Armor secures roads, railheads, oil districts, and towns in Assam, then enables penetration, junction seizure, and rapid exploitation across Bihar. Its utility falls sharply in upland insurgency, flooded ground, and poorly bridged sectors.</p>"},
      {id:"dependency",title:"The brittle supply chain",html:"<p>Japanese 75 mm ammunition, specialized spares, diesel, lubricants, rail transport, bridges, recovery vehicles, mechanics, radios, and supporting artillery sustain the fleet. Coherence creates combat power and a single dangerous line of dependency.</p>"},
      {id:"type16",title:"Limited Type 16 modernization",html:"<p>Before 1963, Japan supplies a limited number of <a href='type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind.html'>Type 16 Shu-Ha</a> tanks. New equipment enters faster than the army can reproduce the complete Japanese training, maintenance, and combined-arms institution around it.</p>"},
      {id:"reform",title:"From matériel advantage to doctrine",html:"<p>Heavy losses in the Northern Indian War lead to the <a href='azad-hind-armoured-school.html'>Armoured School</a>. Recovery, engineer work, radio procedure, artillery timing, and infantry cooperation become the measures of an armored formation rather than tank specifications alone.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Postwar vehicle",label:"Type 16 Shu-Ha in Azad Hind Service"},{href:"azad-hind-armoured-school.html",kicker:"Doctrinal reform",label:"Azad Hind Armoured School"},{href:"delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html",kicker:"Early high-water",label:"Delhi Campaign"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"../landArmamentsReference%20-%20AltWWII.md",label:"Land Armaments Reference — Japanese armored vehicles"}],
    facts:[["Established early tanks","Chi-Nu and Chi-To"],["Established limited heavier tank","Chi-Ri"],["Established provenance","Some vehicles are Eastern Siberian veterans with surviving markings"],["Working veteran nickname","Siberians"],["Working 1954–55 serviceable fleet","120–180"],["Established later development","Limited advanced Japanese tanks and heavy combat losses"],["Working postwar type","Type 16 Shu-Ha"],["Established institutional result","Investment in a tank school"]]
  }),

  "eastern-fortified-belt":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Eastern Fortified Belt",eyebrow:"Working name for the Congress–Azad Hind frontier · from 1957",infoboxTitle:"Eastern fortified belt",infoboxKicker:"Working layered frontier system",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n1u2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 -->After the 1956 Congress counteroffensive, Azad Hind reinforces earlier defensive positions and Congress entrenches opposite them. Over roughly three years the de facto frontier develops dense fortifications likened by the author to the Maginot Line and the Zone Rouge.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>Eastern Fortified Belt</strong> name and a three-layer system of security strip, main defensive zone, and operational rear are compatible engineering reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n1u2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 -->Post-1956 entrenchment by both Congress India and Azad Hind, reinforcement of earlier Azad Hind positions, development through approximately 1960, declining gunfire under armistice, deep defenses and overlapping fields of fire, and later resistance to full-scale assault are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Eastern Fortified Belt title, exact 1957 start, three-layer structure, transport-node and hydrological scheme, separate Congress and Azad doctrines, detailed facilities, human geography, and 1963–65 role are compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Name, boundary, layers, sectors, depth, works, garrisons, and later demarcation remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"The armed armistice",html:"<p>Congress's 1956 counteroffensive reaches the limit of its eastward logistics as Azad Hind reaches prepared ground near Bengal and its established depots. Both armies halt, reorganize, and fortify rather than risk destruction.</p>"},
      {id:"strip",title:"The security strip",html:"<p>The forward zone contains wire, mines, patrol tracks, observation posts, demolished bridges, cleared fields of fire, relocated villages, and small infantry bunkers. Most border incidents and reconnaissance fights occur here.</p>"},
      {id:"main",title:"The main defensive zone",html:"<p>Entrenched towns, railway stations, river and canal strongpoints, artillery shelters, antitank ditches, buried command posts, protected ammunition stores, and prepared infantry positions cover the usable approaches.</p>"},
      {id:"rear",title:"The operational rear",html:"<p>Reserve camps, rail loops, repair workshops, alternate bridges, field hospitals, hardened airfields, and dispersed fuel and ammunition depots allow defenders to absorb a penetration and counterattack it before strategic collapse.</p>"},
      {id:"geography",title:"Fortification by hydrology",html:"<p>Canals, embankments, irrigation works, rail causeways, reservoirs, and seasonal floodplains become military infrastructure. Some low ground is intended to flood during mobilization; raised roads and rails become artillery-covered approaches.</p>"},
      {id:"congress",title:"Congress defensive doctrine",html:"<p>Congress uses fortified infantry and artillery to delay an attacker while engineers, rail troops, reserves, and mobile armored groups seal the penetration. It trades the possibility of immediate victory for the probability of state survival.</p>"},
      {id:"azad",title:"Azad Hind defensive depth",html:"<p>For Azad Hind the belt protects the approaches to Bengal, Calcutta, and the national ammunition system. Positions first prepared as fallback defenses become the foundation from which later limited offensives begin.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"A frontier that becomes a landscape",html:"<p>By 1970 generations live among restricted hills, checkpoint roads, unexploded shells, mines, abandoned fields, military forests, and villages rebuilt away from defense zones. The belt is both a military institution and a permanent human geography.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html",kicker:"Originating campaign",label:"Delhi Campaign"},{href:"second-preparatory-war.html",kicker:"Mobilization phase",label:"Second Preparatory War"},{href:"patna-front.html",kicker:"Urban hinge",label:"Patna Front"}],
    facts:[["Established development","Both sides entrench after the 1956 reversal through approximately 1960"],["Established frontier","Congress India–Azad Hind"],["Established character","Deep defenses and overlapping fields of fire"],["Working name","Eastern Fortified Belt"],["Working layers","Security strip, main defensive zone, and operational rear"],["Working geography","Rivers, canals, railways, and monsoon terrain"],["Name, layers, boundary, works, and garrisons","Open"]]
  }),

  "second-preparatory-war":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Second Preparatory War",eyebrow:"Northern India · mobilization crisis · c. 1960–1963",infoboxTitle:"Second Preparatory War",infoboxKicker:"Mobilization before general war",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n2v3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 -->Around 1960, northern Indian states enter a <strong>Second Preparatory War</strong>: each fortifies, mobilizes, and prepares for an attack it expects another state to begin, producing years of perpetual war footing before general conflict starts around 1963.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A precise mobilization cascade with a fixed first sequence among Hindustan, Congress India, Azad Hind, communist forces, and princely states is compatible causal reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n2v3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 -->The Second Preparatory War name, approximate 1960–63 period, mobilization rather than continuous general combat, multi-state security dilemma, fortification, arms purchases, persistent expectation of attack, and transition into general war around 1963 are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Formal contemporary usage, the exact reserve, militia, and depot program, state-by-state sequence, likely first-shot cascade, and clean transition into a separately named Northern Indian War are compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Opening, declarations, detailed chronology, formations, fronts, and formal terminology remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"name",title:"A war of preparation",html:"<p>Civilians and governments experience reserve call-ups, trench building, requisition, arms purchases, border raids, and transport controls as wartime even without one uninterrupted campaign. The name distinguishes it from the preparations before the 1952–56 offensive.</p>"},
      {id:"cascade",title:"The mobilization cascade",html:"<p>Every buildup is defensible in isolation. Together they convince each government that delay will leave it weaker, creating a self-reinforcing sequence in which readiness becomes evidence of aggressive intent.</p>"},
      {id:"congress",title:"Congress moves between fronts",html:"<p>Congress India must watch Azad Hind in the east, Hindustan and Delhi in the west, and princely or communist pressures to the south. Moving reserves away from one line reassures nobody and alarms the government facing the receiving line.</p>"},
      {id:"hindustan",title:"Hindustan and Delhi",html:"<p>Hindustan strengthens the Delhi approaches and reads Congress concentrations as preparations to recover the capital. Its mobilization then compels Congress to shift forces westward.</p>"},
      {id:"azad",title:"Azad Hind sees an opening",html:"<p>Calcutta interprets Congress transfers as the limited opportunity it has awaited since 1956. The objective is no longer immediate conquest of Delhi, but recovery of towns, straightening of the frontier, and proof that the national mission remains alive.</p>"},
      {id:"others",title:"Communist and princely opportunism",html:"<p>The Telangana People's Republic expands protected zones while larger powers are distracted. Princely armies prepare to secure disputed towns and roads before a mass state can claim them.</p>"},
      {id:"1963",title:"Working 1963 cascade",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n2v3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 --><p>A possible sequence begins with Hindustani concentration, Congress reserve transfer, Azad Hind attack, and opportunistic action elsewhere.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author establishes only that something eventually begins the wider war around 1963 after years of mutual preparation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"meaning",title:"No clean coalitions",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n2v3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:07:57-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind, Congress India, Hindustan, communist forces, and princely states remain potential adversaries across multiple borders.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Their campaigns intersect as a network of wars; they do not settle into two coherent blocs.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"eastern-fortified-belt.html",kicker:"Prepared battlefield",label:"Eastern Fortified Belt"},{href:"northern-indian-war.html",kicker:"General conflict",label:"Northern Indian War"},{href:"hindustan.html",kicker:"Western claimant",label:"Hindustan"}],
    facts:[["Established name","Second Preparatory War"],["Established period","Approximately 1960–1963"],["Established character","Mutual preparation and perpetual war footing before general combat"],["Principal states","Congress India, Azad Hind, and Hindustan"],["Other actors","Communist and princely states"],["Established measures","Fortification, mobilization, and arms procurement"],["Working outcome title","Northern Indian War"],["Opening sequence and formal usage","Open"]]
  }),

  "azad-hind-armoured-school":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Azad Hind Armoured School",eyebrow:"Working Azad Hind Army institution · after 1963–1965",infoboxTitle:"Azad Hind Armoured School",infoboxKicker:"Working national armored-warfare institution",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z3t4a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->Heavy losses with newly received postwar Japanese tanks lead Azad Hind to invest in a tank school instead of assuming that crews can use imported equipment effectively without an institutional learning system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>Azad Hind Armoured School</strong> title, Calcutta–Kharagpur location, and curriculum in crews, radios, infantry, engineers, artillery, recovery, maintenance, and command are compatible institutional design.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-z3t4b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:05:53-06:00 -->Azad Hind's decision to invest in a tank school after suffering heavy losses with advanced Japanese equipment is established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The formal Armoured School name, post-1965 founding, Calcutta–Kharagpur campus, battlefield-loss taxonomy, crew and radio syllabus, combined-arms and recovery curriculum, mixed-fleet doctrine, retained Japanese advisers, and institutional legacy are assistant synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Name, date, location, organization, staff, curriculum, advisers, badges, and branch schools remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Founded from battlefield losses",html:"<p>Azad Hind wins some direct tank engagements yet loses large numbers of Type 16s to mines, artillery, close assault, damaged bridges, recovery failure, and counterattack inside prepared Congress pockets. The army concludes that the formation, not the tank alone, was immature.</p>"},
      {id:"location",title:"The Calcutta–Kharagpur corridor",html:"<p>A likely site combines rail loading, workshops, industrial support, access to the Bengal defensive system, and proximity to national headquarters. The exact campus and formal founding date remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"crews",title:"Crew and radio discipline",html:"<p>Instruction standardizes crew coordination, gunnery under field conditions, radio procedure, command vocabulary, reporting, and movement at night or in monsoon weather.</p>"},
      {id:"combined",title:"Infantry, engineers, and artillery",html:"<p>Tanks learn to move with infantry through villages and trenches, follow mine-clearing and bridging teams, and coordinate with artillery rather than outrunning the support needed to hold a penetration.</p>"},
      {id:"recovery",title:"Recovery as combat",html:"<p>Field maintenance, towing, route clearance, railway loading, spare-parts accounting, and recovery under fire enter the tactical curriculum. A repairable tank abandoned beyond reach is treated as an operational failure.</p>"},
      {id:"mixed",title:"Commanding mixed Japanese fleets",html:"<p>Units may combine older Chi-lineage vehicles with limited Type 16s and different modification standards. The school develops national procedures for assigning roles without pretending every vehicle has identical mobility, protection, or logistical needs.</p>"},
      {id:"advisers",title:"Japanese advisers retained",html:"<p>Japanese instructors remain valuable, but the purpose is no longer indefinite translation of Japanese drills. Azad Hind must develop doctrine for Indian crews, its own railways, its fortifications, its climate, and its limited industrial base.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"The institutional lesson",html:"<p>The school is the clearest constructive result of the 1963–65 defeat. It turns the phrase that a Japanese tank with an Indian crew is not a Japanese division into a program for making an Azad Hind armored formation in its own right.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"azad-hind-armored-forces.html",kicker:"Parent service",label:"Armored Forces of Azad Hind"},{href:"type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Triggering vehicle",label:"Type 16 Shu-Ha in Azad Hind Service"},{href:"northern-indian-war.html",kicker:"Founding war",label:"Northern Indian War"}],
    facts:[["Established decision","Azad Hind invests in a tank school after heavy losses"],["Working period","After the 1963–1965 war"],["Working location","Calcutta–Kharagpur corridor"],["Working purpose","National armored and combined-arms doctrine"],["Working subjects","Crews, radios, infantry, engineers, artillery, maintenance, and recovery"],["Name, date, campus, organization, and commandant","Open"]]
  }),

  "type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind":azadHindArticle({
    title:"Type 16 Shu-Ha in Azad Hind Service",eyebrow:"Working identification · Azad Hind postwar Japanese armor",infoboxTitle:"Type 16 Shu-Ha",infoboxKicker:"Working identification of Japanese tanks in Azad Hind service",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind receives advanced postwar Japanese tanks for the northern war beginning about 1963. The vehicles are technically superior to Congress India's machines, but inexperienced Azad Hind crews suffer heavy losses and the government responds by investing in an armored school.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Identifying those tanks as the <strong>Type 16 Shu-Ha</strong> and reconstructing their transfer, specifications, variants, and exact battlefield performance are compatible technical synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 -->Advanced postwar Japanese tanks in Azad Hind service, their superiority to local machines, inexperienced Azad Hind crews, heavy wartime losses, and the resulting decision to invest in a tank school are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Type 16 Shu-Ha identification, pre-1963 transfer, limited quantity, Japanese 1956 introduction, technical specifications, 1962 Kai changes, detailed comparison with Vijaya, loss mechanisms, and combined-arms interpretation are incorporated extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Model, transfer date, quantity, unit names, variant mix, markings, losses, and surviving inventory remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"vehicle",title:"Working Type 16 identification",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>The equipment reference identifies the candidate vehicle as a Type 16 entering Japanese service in 1956, with a 36-tonne combat weight, four-person crew, 90 mm Type 16 L/55 gun, 750 hp compact diesel, 55 km/h maximum speed, optical rangefinding, and protection against common medium-caliber weapons.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author has not directly selected this model for Azad Hind's wartime force.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kai",title:"Working 1962 Kai variant",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>The proposed Type 16 Shu-Ha Kai increases combat weight to 37 tonnes and adds improved stabilization, an 800 hp diesel, and a revised final drive.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Whether the variant exists in this form or reaches Azad Hind remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"transfer",title:"Postwar Japanese tanks",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind receives advanced postwar Japanese tanks and uses them in the northern war beginning about 1963.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A limited prewar transfer to selected formations while older Japanese-derived vehicles remain in broad service is compatible reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"training",title:"A machine arriving before its institution",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind crews do not have Japanese experience with the advanced tanks and operate them far less effectively than Japanese crews would.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Training months, prior Chi-To, Chi-Ri, or Chi-Ru experience, and deficiencies in recovery, maintenance, radio, and combined-arms organization are plausible explanations rather than established details.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"vijaya",title:"Against the Model 62 Vijaya",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>The Japanese tanks are more advanced and technically superior, but Congress India's <a href='vijaya-tank.html'>National Tank Model 62 or Vijaya</a> is sufficiently comparable to concern Azad Hind.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Congress crew familiarity, local spares and ammunition, and prepared ground converting that familiarity into parity are compatible operational interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"losses",title:"Heavy armored losses",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind loses many of the advanced tanks during the war.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Mines, ditches, artillery, recoilless weapons, infantry attack, bridge failures, unrecovered breakdowns, Vijaya counterattacks, and intact abandonment are assistant-developed loss mechanisms whose relative importance remains open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"judgment",title:"Equipment and formation performance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>The author distinguishes the Japanese tank's technical quality from Azad Hind's ability to employ it.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Interpreting individual tactical success alongside operational failure, and concluding that the imported tank could not compensate for an immature formation, is compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"The Armoured School legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t7q8j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T01:21:00-06:00 --><p>Heavy losses lead Azad Hind to invest in a tank school rather than assume that crews can use imported equipment effectively without a dedicated institution.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The school's formal name, curriculum, readiness measures, and combined-arms doctrine remain working development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...azadHindCoreRelated,{href:"azad-hind-armored-forces.html",kicker:"Operating arm",label:"Armored Forces of Azad Hind"},{href:"vijaya-tank.html",kicker:"Congress opponent",label:"Vijaya"},{href:"northern-indian-war.html",kicker:"Combat history",label:"Northern Indian War"}],
    additionalSources:[{href:"../landArmamentsReference%20-%20AltWWII.md",label:"Land Armaments Reference — Type 16 Shu-Ha specifications"}],
    facts:[["Established operator","Azad Hind receives advanced postwar Japanese tanks"],["Exact model","Open; Type 16 Shu-Ha is a working identification"],["Established comparison","Technically superior to local tanks; Model 62 / Vijaya is comparable"],["Established employment","Inexperienced Azad Hind crews suffer heavy losses"],["Established consequence","Investment in a tank school"],["Type 16 specifications and Kai variant","Working technical extrapolation"],["Transfer date, quantity, units, and inventory","Open"]]
  })
});

const azadHindOverview = window.deepArticles["azad-hind"];
if (azadHindOverview) Object.assign(azadHindOverview, {
  lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind is a heavily mobilized eastern Indian successor state with extensive Japanese matériel support, Calcutta as its administrative capital, Delhi as its claimed proper capital, and Congress India as its principal rival by 1952.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Continuity from the Provisional Government and INA, a planned secular and culturally federal constitution, and bilateral status outside ordinary GEACPS obligations are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
  canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 -->Azad Hind's eastern position, Japanese support, armored advantage, Calcutta administration, Delhi claim and grievance, pre-1952 internal pacification, 1952–56 war and ammunition-driven reversal, later munitions development, 1963–65 war, permanent mobilization, and 1970 and 1991 disaster-security failures are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Institutional lineage, permanent provisional government, constitutional duality, 1948–52 consolidation framework, federal provincial order, planned economy, secular law, bilateral Japanese association, GEACPS status, leadership succession, chambers, borders, and detailed provincial law are extrapolated or open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
  sections:[
    {id:"institutions",title:"Working preterritorial institutions",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>The <a href='provisional-government-free-india.html'>Provisional Government of Free India</a>, <a href='indian-national-army-azad-hind.html'>Indian National Army</a>, Indian Independence League, Azad Hind Dal, Azad Hind Bank, and Rani of Jhansi organization entering India as an existing apparatus is incorporated historical and institutional extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"capitals",title:"Calcutta and Delhi",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Calcutta is the administrative capital and Delhi the claimed proper capital, producing a national grievance.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Ministries, embassies, <em>Chalo Delhi</em>, a provisional title, and constitutional deferral are compatible elaboration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"order",title:"Working political order",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Permanent mobilization makes the army and defense central to society.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Secular national law, centralized political authority, economic planning, and cultural and administrative federalism are incorporated constitutional extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"east",title:"Working eastern territorial settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind secures the Bangladeshi population, subdues the Assam corridor, and holds territory in Orissa reaching communist forces around Sambalpur and the Mahanadi.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A 1948–52 consolidation, Dacca autonomy, corridor administration, and Orissan resource province are incorporated reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"japan",title:"Japanese relationship",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Japan supplies substantial matériel to Azad Hind and later intervenes militarily during the 1970 Congress invasion.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> An eastern-blocker doctrine, bilateral associate status outside ordinary Sphere duties, refusal to defend Burma, and resentment of Japan's Hyderabad relationship are incorporated diplomatic extrapolation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"wars",title:"1952–56 advance and reversal",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Azad Hind advances against Congress India from 1952 through 1955, but the Burmese Federal Crisis interrupts ammunition supply and contributes to failed 1956 attacks and a Congress counteroffensive; Azad Hind later develops domestic munitions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> <a href='delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html'>Delhi Campaign</a> and <a href='eastern-fortified-belt.html'>Eastern Fortified Belt</a> are compatible working labels and institutional reconstructions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"northern",title:"The 1963–65 war",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8i writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>The multi-sided northern war begins around 1963 and reaches an inconclusive strategic condition by 1965. Azad Hind receives advanced Japanese tanks but inexperienced crews suffer heavy losses, prompting investment in a tank school.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A named preparatory war, exact Type 16 identification, formal Armoured School, and combined-arms doctrine are working developments.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
    {id:"disaster",title:"Disaster and frontier security",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-a7y8j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T02:15:00-06:00 --><p>Congress attacks during the 1970 Bhola cyclone while Azad Hind divides forces between relief and defense; in 1991 Azad Hind retains forces at frontier posts for an attack that never comes, worsening the Bangladesh cyclone disaster.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Treating these events as a formal disaster-security state is compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
  ],
  related:[{href:"provisional-government-free-india.html",kicker:"State order",label:"Provisional Government of Free India"},{href:"eastern-consolidation-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Territorial formation",label:"Eastern Consolidation"},{href:"delhi-campaign-1952-1956.html",kicker:"National campaign",label:"Delhi Campaign"},{href:"northern-indian-war.html",kicker:"Major later war",label:"Northern Indian War"},{href:"disaster-relief-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Civil-military system",label:"Disaster Relief in Azad Hind"},{href:"subhas-chandra-bose.html",kicker:"Founding leader",label:"Subhas Chandra Bose"}],
  facts:[["Established state","Azad Hind"],["Established administrative capital","Calcutta"],["Established claimed proper capital","Delhi"],["Established external supporter","Japan"],["Established principal rival by 1952","Congress India"],["Established political condition","Permanent mobilization"],["Formal name, constitutional order, and eastern consolidation","Working reconstruction"],["GEACPS status, final constitution, borders, and leadership","Open"]]
});

// Keep the attributed article in indian-conflicts-article-data.js authoritative.
const northernWarOverview = null;
if (northernWarOverview) Object.assign(northernWarOverview, {
  lead:"The Northern Indian War was the interconnected 1963–1965 conflict that grew from three years of mobilization among Azad Hind, Congress India, Hindustan, the Telangana People's Republic, and princely states. Its defining eastern campaign pitted limited Japanese Type 16 Shu-Ha forces and Azad Hind's strongest-in-theater air arm against Congress fortifications, local logistics, and Model 62 Vijaya tanks, ending without a decisive territorial or political settlement.",
  canon:"The 1960–63 preparatory phase, multi-state mobilization cascade, 1963–65 main war, principal claimants, limited Azad Hind objectives, Type 16 and Vijaya comparison, chemical warfare, heavy Azad Hind armored losses, Armoured School, Congress defensive restraint, limited local changes, and inconclusive result are established. Opening incident, complete campaigns, commanders, casualties, chemical agents, exact borders, and peace protocols remain open.",
  sections:[
    {id:"preparatory",title:"Second Preparatory War, 1960–1963",html:"<p>The <a href='second-preparatory-war.html'>preparatory phase</a> consists of reserve calls, fortification, arms purchases, border fighting, depot construction, and alliance-building. Each government's defensive action becomes another government's evidence that attack is imminent.</p>"},
    {id:"opening",title:"A mobilization cascade becomes war",html:"<p>Hindustani concentration draws Congress reserves west; Azad Hind reads the transfer as an eastern opening; communist and princely forces exploit the wider mobilization. The sides form connected wars rather than two clean coalitions.</p>"},
    {id:"objectives",title:"Azad Hind's limited campaign",html:"<p>Calcutta seeks to break selected fortifications, recover towns lost in 1956, straighten the line, seize railway junctions, and prove the Delhi mission alive. Congress intends to survive without uncovering its western front against Hindustan.</p>"},
    {id:"armor",title:"Type 16 and Vijaya",html:"<p>Limited <a href='type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind.html'>Type 16 Shu-Ha</a> tanks are technically refined, but Congress crews, local ammunition, maintenance, and prepared ground allow the <a href='vijaya-tank.html'>Model 62 Vijaya</a> system to compete effectively.</p>"},
    {id:"system",title:"The defensive system defeats the tank",html:"<p>Azad Hind loses vehicles to mines, ditches, artillery, recoilless weapons, close assault, failed bridges, recovery breakdown, and Vijaya counterattacks. Individual tank victories do not overcome the <a href='eastern-fortified-belt.html'>fortified belt</a>.</p>"},
    {id:"air-chemical",title:"Air power and chemical war",html:"<p>Japanese support gives Azad Hind local air superiority without the ability to clear buried infantry positions. <a href='chemical-warfare.html'>Chemical use</a>—whose first user and exact agent remain disputed—accelerates sealed, cooled, overpressure fighting compartments, detection, medical response, decontamination, and protected route-clearance systems.</p>"},
    {id:"doctrine",title:"The institutional lessons",html:"<p>Heavy losses produce the <a href='azad-hind-armoured-school.html'>Azad Hind Armoured School</a> and a national doctrine of infantry, engineer, artillery, recovery, maintenance, and radio integration. Congress demonstrates that prepared defense and refusal to chase can preserve a multi-front state.</p>"},
    {id:"outcome",title:"The war ends; the long war remains",html:"<p>Congress loses some local positions but survives; Azad Hind fails to break the defensive belt; Hindustan retains Delhi; Telangana and the major princely states endure. After 1965 no principal government expects one final offensive to reunify India.</p>"}
  ],
  related:[{href:"chemical-warfare.html",kicker:"Escalation and taboo",label:"Chemical Warfare"},{href:"chemical-protected-armored-vehicles-india.html",kicker:"Technical legacy",label:"Chemical-Protected Indian Armor"},{href:"second-preparatory-war.html",kicker:"Mobilization phase",label:"Second Preparatory War"},{href:"eastern-fortified-belt.html",kicker:"Principal battlefield",label:"Eastern Fortified Belt"},{href:"type-16-shu-ha-azad-hind.html",kicker:"Azad Hind armor",label:"Type 16 Shu-Ha in Azad Hind Service"},{href:"vijaya-tank.html",kicker:"Congress armor",label:"Vijaya"},{href:"azad-hind-armoured-school.html",kicker:"Doctrinal result",label:"Azad Hind Armoured School"},{href:"patna-front.html",kicker:"Urban hinge",label:"Patna Front"}],
  facts:[["Preparatory phase","Approximately 1960–1963"],["Main war","1963–1965"],["Principal eastern belligerents","Azad Hind and Congress India"],["Other major actors","Hindustan, Telangana, and princely states"],["Noted armor","Type 16 Shu-Ha and Model 62 Vijaya"],["Azad Hind air position","Strongest Japanese-supported air arm in the theater"],["Technical legacy","Chemical protection and national combined-arms doctrine"],["Result","Inconclusive; local changes only"]]
});
